0007-one-sided-fifo-rdma-write.patch
===================================================================
nccl-mesh-plugin @ 19924dcc, on top of 0004, 0005 and 0006.
Files: include/mesh_plugin.h, src/mesh_plugin.c.  +977 / -16 lines.

BUILT AND MEASURED, NOT ADOPTED.  On this hardware it removes RNR retries
completely and changes throughput by nothing, because the ceiling is the
cards' PCIe Gen5 x4 slots and not the flow control -- see docs/06 sections 9
and 10.  It is kept here as an option for a machine where RNR really is the
binding constraint, and it is deliberately NOT part of the upstream pull
request.  Default behaviour is byte-for-byte the two-sided path.


NCCL_MESH_TRANSPORT=send|write (default send = the two-sided SEND/RECV path
this plugin has always used, so the library stays a drop-in). write switches
the data path to the one NCCL's own IB transport uses: the sender owns a small
ring of slots in registered host memory and advertises it in the handshake;
mesh_irecv arms a zero-length RECV and then publishes the receive buffer's
address, rkey and size into that ring with an inline RDMA_WRITE; mesh_isend
waits for a published slot and issues one IBV_WR_RDMA_WRITE_WITH_IMM straight
into the receiver's buffer. Because the sender cannot write before the slot
appears, and the slot is not published before the RECV is armed, an RNR NAK is
unreachable in the steady state instead of merely being made cheap by 0004.

A send whose slot has not arrived is parked on a per-comm FIFO queue and posted
by mesh_send_drain(), which every mesh_test() on that comm runs first. isend
therefore never returns *request = NULL: NCCL's net API allows that and its own
IB plugin relies on it, but the behaviour has not been measured on this stack
and getting it wrong would be a silent hang.

Version handshake, fail-closed. struct mesh_qp_info stays 32 bytes (growing it
would hang an old peer's MSG_WAITALL recv); its two spare bytes now carry a
magic and a wire version, and a fixed 64-byte struct mesh_qp_ext is exchanged
after the base struct only when BOTH ends set the magic. A missing extension in
write mode, a version mismatch, a layout mismatch or a transport-mode mismatch
all refuse the connection with an explicit line rather than writing the wrong
memory later. Against a pre-0007 peer in send mode no extra byte is exchanged
at all.

Connection pooling and async connect are bypassed on the write path: a pool hit
returns without a handshake, so the peer would never learn the FIFO address.

New knobs: NCCL_MESH_TRANSPORT (send), NCCL_MESH_FIFO_DEPTH (128, clamped to
[32,4096] and rounded to a power of two), NCCL_MESH_FIFO_STRICT (1, torn-slot
double check). NCCL_MESH_MIN_RNR_TIMER stays meaningful for the send path.

The RDMA_READ iflush from 0006 is unchanged and still required: the immediate
proves the data reached the receiver's memory, not that the GPU's read path
sees it.

